The app includes two libraries by others, as one dynamic library, AirSQL.app/Contents/Frameworks/libmariadb.3.dylib,
which scripts/build-mariadb.sh builds from pinned, checksummed source releases (the versions are named there):

- MariaDB Connector/C (https://github.com/mariadb-corporation/mariadb-connector-c), the MySQL and MariaDB client
  library. Copyright MySQL AB, Monty Program AB, MariaDB Corporation AB and the other authors named in its source.
  Licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or (at your option) any later version.
- OpenSSL (https://www.openssl.org), linked into it statically for TLS and cryptography. Copyright The OpenSSL Project
  Authors. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

How the licences fit together: libmariadb as shipped contains OpenSSL, and the Apache License 2.0 is compatible with
version 3 of the LGPL but not with version 2.1, so AirSQL uses Connector/C under the LGPL version 3, as its "any later
version" allows. AirSQL's own code links to that library dynamically, which the LGPL allows for a program under other
terms; AirSQL's licence allows changing AirSQL and reverse engineering it to debug such changes.

The library's source: every AirSQL release attaches the exact sources it was built from, Connector/C's
(mariadb-connector-c-<version>-src.tar.gz) and OpenSSL's (openssl-<version>.tar.gz); scripts/build-mariadb.sh holds
every build option and any patch. The same tarballs are at https://archive.mariadb.org/connector-c-<version>/ and
https://github.com/openssl/openssl/releases.

To use a changed libmariadb: build it (scripts/build-mariadb.sh, pointed at your source, or your own build with the
install name @rpath/libmariadb.3.dylib), put it in place of AirSQL.app/Contents/Frameworks/libmariadb.3.dylib, and sign
the library, then the app, again (a release's own signature loads only a library signed by AirSQL's maker), for
example ad hoc:
    codesign --force --sign - AirSQL.app/Contents/Frameworks/libmariadb.3.dylib
    codesign --force --sign - AirSQL.app

